Single-Crystal X-ray Crystallography. Intensities were collected on a Bruker SMART APEX CCD diffractometer with graphitemonochromated Mo $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation $\left(\lambda=0.71073\mathrm{\AA}\right)$ using the SMART and SAINT programs.72,73 The structure was solved by direct methods and refined on $\{{\bar{F}}^{2}$ by full-matrix least-squares methods with SHELXTL version 5.1.74 Non-hydrogen atoms of the ligand backbones were refined anisotropically. Hydrogen atoms within the ligand backbones and the bridging hydroxyl group were fixed geometrically at calculated positions and allowed to ride on the parent non-hydrogen atoms. Crystallographic data for Zn−BCIP1, Zn−BCIP2, and Ho−TCA are given in Table 3.
For $\mathbf{Zn{-}B C I P1}$ and $\mathbf{Zn{-}B C I P}2$ crystals, hydrogen atoms of the water molecules and the hydroxyl groups were found from the different Fourier map but refined by using the riding model with the thermal parameter being fixed at 1.2 times of the oxygen atoms to which they are attached. Several bond distance constraints were used to aid refinement of the BCIP moiety.
For the Ho−TCA crystal, the geometrical constraints of idealized regular polygons were used for several disordered benzene rings, the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ bond distance of the phenyl ring being $1.39\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and the diagonal $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ distance of the phenyl ring being $2.7\check{8}\:\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$
Typical Procedure for the Alkylation of Aldehydes. Catalysis Reactions with $Z n{-}P Y I s$ as Catalysts. The quartz glass tube was purged with a stream of nitrogen, and $0.8~\mathrm{mL}$ of dry THF was added via syringe, followed by the corresponding $1.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathbf{\mathrm{Zn}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{\mathrm{PYI}}$ $\mathrm{\dot{\big0}}.5\mathrm{mol}$ $\%$ ; $1.9\ \mu\mathrm{mol}$ , 0.005 equiv), $66.0~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of diethyl bromomalonate (0.385 mmol, 1.0 equiv), aldehyde (0.769 mmol, 2.0 equiv), and $90.0~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $^{2,6}$ -lutidine $\mathrm{'0.769~\mmol}$ , 2.0 equiv). The resultant mixture was degassed for $10~\mathrm{\min}$ by bubbling nitrogen through the reaction medium. After the reaction mixture was thoroughly degassed, the vial was sealed with Parafilm and placed approximately $8\ \mathrm{cm}$ from a 26 W fluorescent lamp source. After $24~\mathrm{h},$ , the mixture was centrifuged on a Xiangyi TG16-WS centrifuge at $12000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $\ensuremath{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{{min}}}$ . The remaining solid was washed with THF $2\times2~\mathrm{mL}$ ). The solid was dried in air prior to being reused. The supernatant solution was taken out via syringe and concentrated in vacuo. Yields were determined by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR analysis. The reaction mixture was subjected to the workup protocol outlined in the general procedure and purified by flash chromatography using petroleum ether/ $\mathrm{^{\prime}E t_{2}O}$ $(4/1)$ as the eluent to give the title compound as a colorless oil. The enantiomeric excess was determined according to the literature method: $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of the title compound was added to a mixture of $8.5~\mathrm{\mg}$ of (2S,4S)- $\left(+\right)$ -pentanediol $(>99\%$ ee) and $1.5~\mathrm{\mg}$ of $p$ -toluenesulfonic acid hydrate in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{\Psi}}&{{}\cdot1\mathbf{\Psi}\mathrm{mL}}\end{array}$ ). After consumption of the aldehyde was complete (as judged by TLC analysis), the reaction mixture was concentrated in vacuo and the enantiomeric excess of the title compound was determined by the integration of the two $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR signals (both doublets) in $\mathrm{CDCl}_{3}$ arising from the resultant diastereomeric acetals.
Catalysis Reactions with Ho−TCA and MOF-150 as Catalysts. A mixture of the catalyst $(1.9\ \mu\mathrm{mol},$ 0.005 equiv), $66.0~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of diethyl bromomalonate ( $\mathrm{0.39~mmol}$ , 1.0 equiv), $11.6~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{L}{-}\big/\mathrm{D}$ -PYI (76.9 $\mu\mathrm{mol}_{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ , 0.2 equiv), aldehyde (0.77 mmol, 2.0 equiv), and $90.0\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $^{2,6}$ - lutidine (0.77 mmol, 2.0 equiv) was degassed for $10~\mathrm{min}$ by bubbling nitrogen through the reaction medium. After $^{24}\mathrm{~h,}$ the reaction mixture was subjected to the workup protocol outlined in the general procedure and purified by flash chromatography using petroleum ether/ $\mathrm{Et}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(4/1)$ as the eluent to afford the title compound as a colorless oil. The enantiomeric excess was also determined according to the above method.
